course: dictionary
lesson: 2417
title: Cyanobacteria: tiny living things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Cyanobacteria is a long word for tiny living things. They are a kind of bacteria, so small that you need a microscope to see one.
explain: Like plants, they make their own food from sunlight, and this is called photosynthesis. «Billions of years ago, cyanobacteria made the first oxygen in the air by photosynthesis.» So the context tells you that the word names a way of using light to make food.
explain: You may hear them called blue-green algae. When a lake has a «blue-green algae» warning sign, cyanobacteria may be in the water. They can be poisonous to people and dogs, so do not swim there.
fact: Cyanobacteria are tiny living things, a kind of bacteria.
fact: Like plants, they make their own food from sunlight.
fact: Sometimes called blue-green algae, they live in water and can turn ponds and lakes green.
fact: An «algal bloom» is a big growth of these tiny living things in water, which can turn the water green.
fact: Cyanobacteria were among the first living things to make oxygen, which helped make Earth's air breathable for animals.
exercise: What are cyanobacteria? | A type of fish that lives in ponds | *Tiny living things, a kind of bacteria | Cold winds that blow over lakes | Large rocks found on the seabed
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Billions of years ago, cyanobacteria made the first oxygen in the air by photosynthesis» that names how they make food from sunlight. | oxygen | ancient | *photosynthesis | billions
exercise: A lake has a «blue-green algae» warning sign. Why should you not swim there? | The lake is too salty to swim in | *Cyanobacteria in the water may be poisonous to people and dogs | The water is too cold for swimming | Fish might bite your toes
test: What did cyanobacteria help make in Earth's air? | *Oxygen | Sand dunes | Ice crystals | Rain clouds
test: A pond turns bright green after a warm summer. What is the most likely cause? | A boat sailing across it | Snow falling on the pond | *Cyanobacteria growing in the water | Salt from the sea
exam: Which statement about cyanobacteria is true? | They only live in the sea and never in ponds | *They make their own food from sunlight, like plants do | They are big animals that eat fish | They are a type of rock that grows in Shark Bay
